Subject: The unit owns its contracted layouts, so a field has a constant slot

What this does
  - `JSShape` gains `layout_id`, a uint16 that names the contracted layout a shape
    ends, or 0 for a shape no layout owns.
  - The runtime gains the layouts' shapes, their count, and a word of bits recording
    which layouts have been broken by an object leaving them.
  - An object adopting any other shape leaves the layout it was in, so
    `js_aot_layout_leave` sets that layout's bit. It tests only the shape being left,
    because entering a contracted shape is never a break. It is called wherever an
    object's shape is replaced: both clone branches of the in-place update paths, the
    property-add path, compaction, the two compiled add-transition paths and the JSON
    adoption path. Object construction and the free path have no previous shape and
    are not hooked, and neither is `resize_properties`, which only runs on a shape
    whose reference count is one -- a contracted shape never is.
  - At most 63 layouts are emitted (the hottest by site count; the rest stay
    uncontracted), and the bind path asserts the table holds no more, so the bit word
    cannot silently drop a break past id 63.
  - `qjs_aot_release(JSRuntime *)` is the unit's teardown, called once at the start of
    `JS_FreeRuntime` before the final collection. It drops the references the unit's
    caches hold: its site callees and the shapes of its contracted layouts. The unit
    records the runtime that filled those caches and releases only for that runtime,
    so a value of one runtime is never freed with another.
  - Two accessors let the unit take and drop a reference on a shape without naming
    `JSShape` or `js_free_shape`, and one reports a shape's layout id.
  - The layout export gains the five new offsets.

Why we need it
  The compiled code cannot read a field at a constant slot while a shape may change
  underneath it: QuickJS mutates a shape in place when its reference count is one, so
  the property cache cell is the only authority on a field's offset, and every access
  reloads it. A shape the unit holds a reference to is never mutated in place, since
  both in-place paths clone above a count of one. Contracting a layout therefore turns
  its field offsets into facts: the compiled body reads a constant slot, and the
  contract is broken -- once, permanently, like a map deprecation -- if any object is
  seen to leave the layout. The bit is checked at function entry, not per access.

Functions touched
  `js_aot_layout_leave` (new), `js_shape_prepare_update`, `add_property`,
  `compact_properties`, `regvm_put_field_add_hit`, the put_field path in
  `JS_CallInternal`, `json_obj_adopt_shape`, `JS_FreeRuntime`, and the new
  `qjs_aot_release`, `qjs_aot_layout_dup`, `qjs_aot_layout_free` and
  `qjs_aot_shape_layout`.

No (the bit is 0 and no layout is contracted without a unit that declares one; with
no unit every hook tests a zero field and the release is not linked).

